{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_24", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n 'center|gauge|log|potential' /tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_7_continuing_notes.md\"", "aggregated_output": "/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:19:| 460_episode8_shared_initial_gauge | 0.041269054201742011 | 0.92086389287229342 | fab4ae464687ac8e3c80604ccd4be38b66476d47e031b62425cc7300a9f253a5 |\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:20:| 473_episode8_virtual_combine_point_last_logcolumn | 0.045238594365946481 | 0.91325204031694796 | fc9b3e4401eea47ac5d9df307a79d703e8a1bf9e0ea5f759c19ff992e6475cfb |\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:21:| 490_episode8_mean_first_combine_shared_point_logcolumn | 0.040081313966463007 | 0.92314146235666883 | 1ff73d90ffb05ae31910ecc236b7481fdc268b37573cd916036dd2058015cb90 |\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:30:- **458–460/483, shared initialization and cost gauge:** derive all six initial point potential pairs from shared batch means, label frequencies and norms, avoiding dense cost means. Equivalent unary cost recentering and skipping zero-dual subtraction also pass all 30. Full 460 raw 0.041269 loses to historical best.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:32:- **467/468/484/485, final point column cancellation:** keep final row scaling and powers, then move the final column factor into vector logarithms before updating g. Zero sums use the stable dense column logsumexp fallback. All 30 pass with essentially base accuracy; 484 adds shared analytic means, and 485 adds half-folding. These point changes are algebraic reorganizations.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:40:Two fresh-process benchmark rounds used eight independent processes per source in alternating order. Round 1 favored shared initialization/gauge and exact point rearrangements; round 2 favored cached-class/log-column 473 and mean-first/shared-point 490. Raw timing lists are preserved in `episode8_fresh_round1.log` and `episode8_fresh_round2.log`. They are not evaluator scores.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:44:The starting cProfile overlapped one local screen and includes NPZ argument reads in its total; it is explicitly diagnostic. It identified class transforms and point kernel operations as the remaining costs. The class-stage instrumented source/log shows no soft fallback on public 303 Gaussian016: temperatures about 3024–9829, 756–2457, 189–614, then hard stages down to 0.738–2.400.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:50:- Builders round 1–10, manifests, full per-case screen logs/JSON, failed sources, original driver before the import-error fix, actual 464 compile stderr, evaluator reports/stdout, profiles and fresh timing lists remain under `/tmp/research_history`.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:51:- Read prior episode 7/6/5/4 summaries before revisiting segmented NumPy class solves, generic grouping, barycenters, lower precision, polynomial coupling or Gaussian point approximations; they generally failed or were slower. This episode confirms that extending fixed class minimizer graphs over several finite annealing steps is inaccurate.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_8_continuing_notes.md:52:- Next work should profile the selected source first. Exact point log-column cancellation is stable and available as a building block. Class covariance approximation is accurate but expensive even with unique supports. A materially different fused class reduction or compressed geometry mechanism would be more promising than further extrapolation constants. Global Torch segment reductions remain a possible new mechanism; previous global segmented **NumPy** versions were slow, so validate the kernel cost before implementing a complete variant.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_7_continuing_notes.md:12:Class transport still