{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"cat > /tmp/research_history/episode12_derivations.md <<'EOF'\n# Episode 12 mechanisms\n\nEvery computation below is derived from the supplied arrays, preserving all first-batch empirical supports and the original contiguous point batch partition. Local screens use own canonical public-array calculations saved in previous research, never protected targets. Official evaluation is only through the frozen launcher.\n\n## Implicit separable kernel factors\n\nIf the explicit kernel is K=diag(u) H diag(v), a finite parallel update at temperature ratio q has Knew=(K * sqrt(m/rs)[:,None] * sqrt(n/cs)[None,:])**q. It can instead update Hnew=H**q, unew=(u*sqrt(m/rs))**q, vnew=(v*sqrt(n/cs))**q, then obtain rs=unew*(Hnew @ vnew), cs=vnew*(Hnew.T @ unew). Holding only u or only v implicitly saves one dense multiplication; the other scaling is still applied to H before powers. Both factors can be normalized by maxima, merging the compensating scalar into H before powers. Logarithmic and purely algebraic normalization controls are archived. Stable direct transforms reset implicit factors to ones when marginals are nonfinite or too small.\n\nThe final point stage only needs the reference update. The previous column balancing factor is moved into q*log(v)+.5*q*(log(n)-log(cs)), while remaining row factors are raised to q and contracted with H**q. This preserves the selected finite recipe; it is a representation change, not a converged Sinkhorn substitute.\n\n## Shared empirical first-batch geometry\n\nFull first candidate/reference feature geometry is G=-X @ R.T+||X||²/2+||R||²/2, equivalently a 12-dimensional homogeneous product. Padded class-pair indices gather G while preserving every support's input order. The original G can later be used for the first point pair before adding empirical label costs. Controls use Torch advanced indexing, two-axis np.take, or reconstruct unpadded G from the original class BMM instead. The latter trades a product for permutations and indexed copies.\n\n## Grouped exact coupling contractions\n\nEach original point pair retains its own beta=max(C)/10 and independent uniform marginals. After building a full kernel whose blocks have their own beta, blocked response columns encode each pair's v/u/w values with zeros outside the appropriate supports. GEMMs produce the same row, column and weighted-energy contractions as independent scalar response GEMMs. A global6-column version multiplies many zeros; a blocked variant uses2 reference responses per candidate block and3 candidate responses per reference block. Pair cost expectations and n+m divisors are unchanged. Original point-potential loops run after coupling on untouched full costs copied per pair.\n\n## Full hard-suffix cache blocking\n\nAfter soft class stages, form resident reduced cost R=C-f-g. All class pairs are independent. Loop interchange runs every retained hard stage and both final transforms within one block of10/20/25/50/100 pairs before advancing to the next block. Each uses df=rowmin(R)-eps*lb, dg=colmin(R)-eps*la and the same parallel dual increments. It preserves all masks, per-pair temperatures, final .25 reference extrapolation and final empirical objective. This differs from earlier per-stage kernel tiling: the entire late suffix remains in a block's working set.\n\n## Native fused final contractions\n\nThe final point kernel is discarded after a column sum. A NumPy einsum with four identical operands computes sum_i H_ij\"'^4 in one native contraction after row balancing, eliminating dense squared-kernel writes. A five-operand version also includes (m/rs_i)''^2, avoiding the dense row-balancing write. Weight-first and float64 accumulation controls distinguish multiplication-order/range effects. Nonfinite or zero sums retain the existing logsumexp fallback. Coupling energy similarly contracts K_ij*C_ij*w_j using a native three-operand einsum, avoiding a dense K*=C pass. These are exact algebraic identities with float32 reduction-order differences.\nEOF'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
